# Lesson 3, new exercise: knock out the ace of diamonds while you still hold a spade stopper.
title: Force out the ace first
id: L03-07
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.2, 3.4
student: S
board: 39
dealer: S
vulnerable: both

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  862    K73    KQJT6  Q2
  E  53     QJT4   982    KT98
  S  AK4    A852   73     AJ64
  W  QJT97  96     A54    753

auction:
  S 1NT*   = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 16 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 9-10
  spades: 2 = The ace and king.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king.
  diamonds: 4 = Four, once the ace is gone. Force it out first.
  clubs: 1-2 = The ace. The queen might win too, depending on where the king is.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit.
  remind: Dummy's diamonds have no winners on top. How many will they have once the ace is gone?
play:
  1   W SQ  N S2  E S3  S SA
      note: Only five winners on top: two spades, two hearts and the ♣A. But once the ♦A is gone, dummy's diamonds are worth four tricks. Win the first spade and keep the king to stop spades a second time.
  2   S D3  W DA  N D6  E D2
      note: Start on diamonds right away. The ace is going to win a trick whatever you do, so let it win now.
  3   W SJ  N S6  E S5  S SK
      note: West keeps on with spades, and your king stops them.
  4   S D7  W D4  N DK  E D8
      note: Now cross to dummy's diamonds. They are all winners.
  5   N DQ  E D9  S C4  W D5
      note: Keep cashing dummy's diamonds.
  6   N DJ  E C8  S H2  W S7
      note: Another winner.
  7   N DT  E C9  S C6  W S9
      note: The last diamond.
  8   N HK  E H4  S H5  W H6
      note: Now take your other winners. Dummy's ♥K first, the high card from the short side...
  9   N H3  E HT  S HA  W H9
      note: ...then the ♥A in your hand.
  10  S CA  W C3  N C2  E CT
      note: And the ♣A makes nine tricks.
  11  S CJ  W C5  N CQ  E CK
      note: Nine tricks are in. The rest belong to the defense.
  12  E HQ  S H8  W C7  N H7
  13  E HJ  S S4  W ST  N S8
      note: 9 tricks: 3NT made.

play recap:
  The plan: count five winners on top, then get four more from dummy's
  diamonds by forcing out the ace.

  West had three spade winners set up by the end, but never got back in to
  take them. You forced out the ♦A while you still had a spade stopper.
